----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, Q: d8-d5, Q: d8-d4, Q: d8-d3, Q: d8-d2, Q: d8-e7, K: e8-e7, N: f6-d5, N: f6-d7, N: f6-e4, N: f6-g4, N: f6-g8, N: f6-h5, P: f7-e6, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: f8-b4, B: f8-a3, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: The position is fairly closed with pawns locked in the center. Black has a well-developed knight on f6 and pieces ready to emerge. White's knight on c3 is developed, but Black can challenge it. Black's main objectives should be to complete development, control the center, and create potential threats.  \n\nPromising moves:  \n- **P: g7-g5**: This prepares to expand on the kingside, but weakens the kingside pawn structure and may expose the king after castling kingside. Risky without king safety.  \n- **P: c7-c5**: Strikes at the center and opens lines for the queen and bishop. Good for development and central control.  \n- **N: f6-e4**: Attacks the white pawn on e5, but the pawn is protected (by the d4 pawn indirectly and knight on c3). Not immediately winning material, but could provoke weaknesses.  \n- **B: f8-c5**: Develops the bishop to an active diagonal, targeting f2 and supporting the center. This is a solid developing move.  \n- **Q: d8-d7**: Develops the queen, but blocks the bishop on c8. Less flexible than other options.  \n\nReason: **P: c7-c5** is a classic Sicilian-style break that challenges White's central control, opens the c-file for the rook, and activates the black queen and bishop. It also avoids weakening the king's position prematurely. Given that Black’s knight on f6 is already developed and the dark-squared bishop can go to b4 or c5 next, this move fits best with opening principles and puts immediate pressure on White.  \n\nAnswer: P: c7-c5"}
