ailable for king later? King already moved (KBF1) so castling not an issue. But rook on e8 might be exposed to White’s bishop on e5.  \n5. **P: c5-c4** – Gains space and threatens to push pawn to c3 disrupting White’s structure, but White’s knight on c3 is already there, so c4 pawn could be weak.\n\nThe most solid and improving move is **B: e7-d6**. It removes the bishop from blocking the queen’s diagonal, breaks the pin on the rook (since rook on f8 is no longer pinned if bishop moves), and keeps Black’s position flexible.\n\n**Reason:**  \nBy playing **B: e7-d6**, Black solves the immediate tactical vulnerability (the pin on the f8 rook) and develops the bishop to a more active diagonal. This avoids potential future tactics from White moving the bishop on e5 with discovered attacks. It also prepares potential ...e5 push later to challenge the center. Other moves either weaken the king’s position (g5) or don’t address the pin issue.\n\n**Answer:** B: e7-d6'}
